#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "751c92b4-3b48-546b-902e-6679a757225c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278571} {:answer/id #uuid "e80728a9-3025-5675-8bbd-4f6c11fc4c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278572} {:answer/id #uuid "485d1a06-bc45-55d7-bc84-3ff478f6c358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278573} {:answer/id #uuid "41e81461-54b6-55ff-bb29-74298379cdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278574} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2cea99-320f-5686-80a6-703358b8b96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "751c92b4-3b48-546b-902e-6679a757225c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278571} :answer-field/id #uuid "1375ee2f-e69d-5865-9f2a-987fca463960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2dbc0e3f-52e0-5c91-bb08-00ee163a5979" :question/math-academy-id "q-117412" :question/problem "$10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978511/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25249a7c-b142-5efb-be44-5364af701443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278578} {:answer/id #uuid "af02623f-df1e-52b6-a8fb-09b2d97936df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278579} {:answer/id #uuid "e4883042-5eb1-59fb-9a98-e458ad0d7f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278580} {:answer/id #uuid "5202e221-fe75-56ae-a88a-eb7907338342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278581} {:answer/id #uuid "d03ca6d6-79c7-53da-b39b-c3fccb4c8c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5202e221-fe75-56ae-a88a-eb7907338342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278581} :answer-field/id #uuid "230471ac-8884-5482-99f0-e42d06daa657" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c851664f-56c5-5c6e-824e-5a13456731a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-117456" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡({45}^{∘})=1.\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)={45}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fea6824-130d-568a-8b22-651d4b7f4508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fea6824-130d-568a-8b22-651d4b7f4508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278585} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6baa3ed-3895-59e1-a671-f7ca80a57681" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f53bc9c3-fd24-580b-be8a-fd85b0a8922e" :question/math-academy-id "q-320730" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={-30}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521e0961-320b-53aa-8dcf-420ec8d76bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278588} {:answer/id #uuid "824397f3-bb67-56d1-9fe3-65a62945f249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278589} {:answer/id #uuid "85559168-79fb-5d5e-9ce9-8b5b8810fc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278590} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dc6b74-f8e5-5c2f-92c3-73897e47ed5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278591} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2979e6-60ed-5dcb-838e-b5a4303b5e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2dc6b74-f8e5-5c2f-92c3-73897e47ed5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278591} :answer-field/id #uuid "caf0c747-d226-5dd8-89fb-ae2aa8599de0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b74ba82-3450-586d-bec9-151c983d04a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82179" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978511/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({300}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${300}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${300}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e2bbeb-d4ba-5a72-bd16-ab326668c099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278595} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5001de-9a28-5808-8027-873889be49c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278596} {:answer/id #uuid "3368aef2-4b23-595d-8b16-5494e6310577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278597} {:answer/id #uuid "b09a1d17-3f9e-5902-8c52-64e3ac9f8569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278598} {:answer/id #uuid "eac8d1d3-4681-5070-ac67-8bcc76358108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f5001de-9a28-5808-8027-873889be49c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278596} :answer-field/id #uuid "79c2aeb9-319e-5127-9c2b-5880ffe16005" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e68a9882-b279-53d3-a7c6-3e64592c9480" :question/math-academy-id "q-90536" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978511/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
